\section{Experiments}

We apply the G-theory framework from Section~3 to a fully crossed evaluation design spanning multiple models, benchmarks, and methodological facets. All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96GB). Binary correctness (1/0) serves as the primary outcome measure throughout; generation-based scoring avoids the log-likelihood artifacts identified by \citet{hua2025flaw}.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}

We first establish the variance structure within a single model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) on MMLU to isolate methodological facet contributions before introducing cross-model variation.

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 259,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Under binary scoring, item difficulty (\texttt{item\_id}) accounts for 58.4\% of total variance, with the residual term contributing 23.1\%. All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each): precision 0.9\%, prompt template, temperature, seed, and ordering each $<$0.5\%. The interesting structure appears in item-conditional interactions: prompt$\times$item is the largest at 5.5\%, followed by seed$\times$item (4.7\%), precision$\times$item (3.3\%), temperature$\times$item (2.9\%), and ordering$\times$item (0.8\%). This pattern indicates that methodological choices do not shift performance uniformly across items; instead, specific items are differentially sensitive to specific facets---prompt template variation produces the largest item-conditional effect.

The resulting generalizability coefficient is $G_\text{item} = 0.936$, indicating that 200 items under multi-facet conditions produce highly reliable rankings. A D-study projection shows $G \geq 0.80$ is achievable with as few as 55 items in this multi-facet design.

For text-exact-match scoring (requiring verbatim string matches), the variance structure shifts dramatically: temperature dominates at 79.7\% of variance, and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007. This metric-conditional divergence motivates our exclusive use of binary correctness for the remaining analyses.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

We extend the single-model design to eight models spanning eight architecture families. A balanced BF16 subset (460,800 records) serves as the primary analysis sample, chosen because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures. The eight-model sample strengthens the quasi-random model facet assumption by covering a broader population of instruction-tuned 7--9B architectures.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} presents the variance decomposition. Introducing model as a facet reshapes the structure: item difficulty drops from 58.4\% to 37.9\%, while model$\times$item interaction emerges as the second-largest component at up to 36\%. The model main effect accounts for 3--5\% on MC benchmarks and 9--11\% on free-form benchmarks, confirming that models differ primarily in \emph{which items} they find difficult rather than in overall ability level.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Variance decomposition for the cross-model MMLU design (8 models, BF16, 460,800 records). Components estimated via Henderson Method~I. $G_\text{item} = 0.896$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Component & Var.\% \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{37.9} \\
\underline{model$\times$item} & \underline{30.2} \\
residual & 25.0 \\
model & 2.8 \\
prompt$\times$item & 2.5 \\
model$\times$prompt & 1.6 \\
all others & $<$1.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The per-model decomposition of interaction magnitude reveals meaningful asymmetry across eight models, suggesting that instruction-tuning strategies and pretraining data composition produce model-specific item difficulty profiles.

\paragraph{D-study validation.} The eight-model sample validates the quasi-random model facet assumption. D-study projections from four-model subsets predicted $G = 0.893$ at eight models; the observed $G = 0.896$ confirms the projection ($\Delta = 0.003$).

\subsection{Format-Conditional Variance Structure}
\label{sec:format_conditional}

The preceding analyses use only MMLU, a multiple-choice benchmark. We now test whether the item-dominated variance structure generalizes across evaluation formats by extending to five benchmarks spanning two response formats.

\paragraph{Design.} The expanded design (exp-002) crosses 8 models $\times$ 5 benchmarks $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 2,304,000 records. The benchmarks include three multiple-choice tasks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag) and two free-form generation tasks (GSM8K, MATH). The ordering facet operates only on MMLU (four demo-example permutations); for other benchmarks it is structurally inert ($\sigma^2_{\text{ordering}} = 0$) and retained for design balance.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reveals a systematic format-dependent pattern. For MMLU and ARC-Challenge, item difficulty dominates (37.9\% each), with model$\times$item second (30.2\% and 33.7\%). HellaSwag reverses this ordering: model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item difficulty (34.3\%), indicating maximal model disagreement at the item level.

Free-form benchmarks differ markedly. On GSM8K, variance distributes across item (16.9\%), model$\times$item (14.6\%), model (10.7\%), and model$\times$prompt (8.5\%); prompt main effect is 5.4\%. MATH shows an intermediate pattern: item leads at 24.0\% with model$\times$item at 17.3\%, but residual variance is substantially larger (43.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-conditional variance structure across five benchmarks (exp-002, 8 models, 2,304,000 records). Variance components (\%) per benchmark; MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form. Bold indicates the dominant non-residual component per benchmark. For MC benchmarks, ``prompt'' reports prompt$\times$item (prompt main effect $<$1\%); for FF benchmarks, it reports the prompt main effect.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{l rrr rr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{FF} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
 & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM & MATH \\
\midrule
\textbf{item} & \textbf{37.9} & \textbf{37.9} & 34.3 & 16.9 & 24.0 \\
mod.$\times$item & 30.2 & 33.7 & \textbf{36.0} & 14.6 & 17.3 \\
model & 2.8 & 3.1 & 5.4 & 10.7 & 8.5 \\
prompt & 2.5 & 0.4 & 2.3 & 5.4 & 1.0 \\
mod.$\times$prmpt & 1.6 & 8.0 & 0.6 & 8.5 & 2.0 \\
residual & 25.0 & 15.4 & 21.4 & 39.9 & 43.9 \\
all others & $<$1.0 & 1.5 & $<$1.0 & 4.0 & 3.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The variance structure is format-conditional: MC benchmarks concentrate variance in items and model$\times$item interaction (34--38\% and 30--36\%), while FF benchmarks distribute it across item, model, and interaction components (5--24\% each) with larger residuals (40--44\%). A permutation test of the MC versus FF item-variance difference yields $p = 0.10$ ($n = 5$ benchmarks, $\binom{5}{3} = 10$ exhaustive permutations), the theoretical minimum for this sample size. The effect size is large (Cohen's $d = 1.73$; mean difference 95\% CI [11.44, 21.02] percentage points), though benchmark-level generalization uncertainty is substantial given $n = 5$. This structural difference does not extend to item budgets: HellaSwag requires 171 items for $G \geq 0.80$, the highest of all benchmarks, showing that format alone does not predict evaluation cost. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast.

To probe the mechanism behind item variance, we exploit the natural heterogeneity within MMLU's 57 subjects. Subjects with higher item difficulty dispersion (measured by $\text{std}(p_i)$ across models) exhibit higher item variance shares, with Spearman $\rho = 0.806$ ($p = 0.005$, $n = 10$ subjects). Notably, \texttt{high\_school\_mathematics}, the only MC subject with open-ended numerical items, shows item variance of just 10.4\%---below the free-form benchmark GSM8K (16.9\%)---suggesting that item heterogeneity, not format per se, drives variance allocation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition (Henderson Method~I) across five benchmarks and eight models. Multiple-choice benchmarks share an item-dominated structure; HellaSwag is the exception where model$\times$item (36.0\%) exceeds item difficulty (34.3\%). Free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH) distribute variance across item, model, and interaction components with no single dominant source.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Prior work has identified numerical precision as a potential source of evaluation variance \cite{yuan2025layercast}. Our multi-facet design allows us to isolate the precision contribution while controlling for all other facets simultaneously.

Under binary scoring with BF16 and FP16, the mean accuracy difference is 0.23 percentage points. After excluding FP32, the precision main effect drops to 0.0006\% of total variance. The FP32 anomaly traces to a vLLM implementation detail (\texttt{enforce\_eager} mode required for FP32 inference), not a fundamental precision effect.

Precision does not interact with other methodological facets under binary scoring: all precision$\times$methodology interactions contribute $<$0.1\% of variance. Under text-exact-match scoring, however, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with the metric-conditional pattern observed in Section~4.1. The practical implication is clear: for standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16, numerical precision is a negligible variance source that need not be controlled. This finding is specific to single-GPU inference and does not address multi-GPU tensor parallelism, where numerical non-associativity introduces additional variance sources \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

The previous sections establish that item selection and model$\times$item interaction dominate evaluation variance. A natural question follows: how many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We address this through D-study projections that translate variance components into concrete item budgets.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares four evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold. The multi-facet design (1,296 conditions per item: 3 precisions $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 3 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings) achieves $G = 0.936$ at 200 items and requires only 55 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design (one fixed prompt, seed, ordering, and precision per item) reaches only $G = 0.592$ at 200 items and requires 552 items for the same threshold. The ratio between these budgets is 10.0$\times$, and this ratio remains stable across subset sizes from 50 to 200 items (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs. The multi-facet design requires 10.0$\times$ fewer items than single-condition evaluation to reach $G \geq 0.80$.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcrrr}
\toprule
Design & Cond. & $n_{G \geq 0.80}$ & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 1,296 & \textbf{55} & \textbf{0.936} & --- \\
Single-condition & 1 & 552 & 0.592 & 0.457 \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & --- & 116 & --- & 0.457$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT item count of 116 \cite{zhou2026irt} evaluated under single-condition protocol yields $G = 0.457$. See text for interpretation.}
\end{table}

The comparison with IRT-based efficient benchmarking is instructive. \citet{zhou2026irt} use IRT to select 116 informative items from MMLU, optimizing for item discrimination. When we evaluate these 116 items under single-condition protocols, the resulting $G = 0.457$ falls well below the reliability threshold. This is not a failure of IRT item selection; rather, it reflects that IRT optimizes a different objective (latent trait estimation) that does not account for methodological facet variance. IRT selects items optimized for trait discrimination; multi-facet G-study improves reliability orthogonally by averaging over methodological noise sources that IRT does not model. Under the multi-facet design, 116 items also exceed $G = 0.80$, confirming that the reliability gap is driven by evaluation design, not item quality.

Figure~\ref{fig:dstudy} plots the D-study projection curves for each design. The multi-facet curve saturates rapidly (diminishing returns beyond $\sim$150 items), while the single-condition curve rises slowly and does not reach $G = 0.80$ within typical benchmark sizes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_dstudy_curves}
\caption{D-study projection curves for three evaluation designs. The multi-facet design (10.0$\times$ fewer items) reaches $G \geq 0.80$ at 55 items; the single-condition design requires 552 items. The shaded band shows 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{fig:dstudy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

We conduct six robustness analyses to verify that the reported variance structures are not artifacts of specific analytical choices. \textbf{Item resampling} (exp-001b): three independent 200-item samples drawn from the full MMLU pool and evaluated under a BF16-only 5-facet design yield item difficulty variance shares of 73.2\%, 72.6\%, and 69.0\% (CV = 3.1\%) and $G_\text{item}$ values with CV = 0.5\%. The higher item share relative to the 6-facet baseline (58.4\%) reflects the absence of the precision facet and its item interactions. Five additional robustness analyses (estimator comparison, CI method, facet specification, leave-one-model-out, and null calibration) are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}; all confirm the stability of the reported variance structure.
